.TH FMT 3
.SH NAME
fmt \- string formatting routines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.ft CR
#include <ast.h>
#include <ls.h>

char*      fmtbuf(size_t \fIsize\fP);
char*      fmtclock(Sfulong_t \fItics\fP);
char*      fmtdev(struct stat* \fIst\fP);
char*      fmtelapsed(unsigned long \fIcount\fP, unsigned long \fIpersec\fP);
char*      fmtesc(const char* \fIstring\fP);
char*      fmtfmt(const char* \fIformat\fP);
char*      fmtfs(struct stat* \fIst\fP);
char*      fmtgid(gid_t \fIgid\fP);
char*      fmtident(const char* \fIstring\fP);
char*      fmtint(intmax_t \fInumber\fP, int \fIunsign\fP);
char*      fmtip4(uint32_t \fIaddr\fP, int \fIbits\fP);
char*      fmtmatch(const char* \fIre\fP);
char*      fmtmode(mode_t \fImode\fP, int \fIexternal\fP);
char*      fmtnum(unsigned long \fInumber\fP, int \fIdivide\fP);
char*      fmtperm(mode_t \fIperm\fP);
char*      fmtre(const char* \fIpattern\fP);
char*      fmtscale(Sfulong_t \fInumber\fP, unsigned int \fIscale\fP);
char*      fmtsignal(int \fIsig\fP);
char*      fmttime(const char* \fIformat\fP, time_t \fItm\fP);
char*      fmtuid(uid_t \fIuid\fP);
.ft
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These routines return a pointer to a formatted string for various numeric
and string entities.
Some routines may cache information to speed up the next call.
Most of the routines return a pointer to a private buffer, the
contents of which are overwritten on subsequent calls;
see
.B fmtbuf
below.
Most
\f[CB]fmt\fP
routines have a corresponding
\f[CB]str\fP
routine that converts in the other direction.
There is nothing spectacular about this collection other than that
it provides a single place where the exact format is spelled out.
.PP
\f[CB]fmtbuf\fP
returns a pointer to a temporary buffer of
.I size
bytes.
It is used by the routines described below to hold their formatted results,
but can also be used by any other code that needs a buffer.
Buffers smaller than 16 KiB are allocated within a single static buffer
of that size. Their overwriting by subsequent calls is delayed as long
as psosible by cycling through the available space within this buffer.
Larger requests are satisfied by a single dynamically allocated buffer that
is re-used and overwritten on each call requesting a buffer >= 16 KiB.
.PP
\f[CB]fmtclock\fP
formats the
.BR clock (3)
elapsed tick count
.I tics
into a human-readable string of at most 6 characters.
Values are expressed in microseconds, milliseconds, or
\f[CB]fmtelapsed\fP
units as appropriate.
\f[CB]0\fP is returned if
.IR "tics == 0" ,
and
\f[CB]%\fP
if
.IR "tics == ~(Sfulong_t)0" .
.PP
\f[CB]fmtdev\fP
returns the device handle name specified by the
\f[CB]stat\fP
structure
.IR st .
This is the device information displayed by
.IR "ls \-l" .
.PP
\f[CB]fmtelapsed\fP
formats the elapsed time for
.I (count/persec)
seconds.
The two largest time units are used, limiting the return value length
to at most 6 characters.
The units are:
.RS
.TP
.B s
seconds
.TP
.B m
minutes
.TP
.B h
hours
.TP
.B d
days
.TP
.B w
weeks
.TP
.B M
months
.TP
.B Y
years
.RE
.IP
\f[CB]0\fP is returned if
.IR "count == 0" ,
and
\f[CB]%\fP
if
.IR "count == ~0UL" .
.PP
\f[CB]fmtesc\fP
formats non-ASCII characters in
.I string
into C-style
.B \ee
sequences.
These sequences are understood by
\f[CB]chresc\fP
and
\f[CB]chrtoi\fP.
.PP
\f[CB]fmtfmt\fP
returns the
.BR printf (3)
format signature of
.IR format :
a string containing one character per conversion specification,
each preceded by the number of
.B *
arguments if greater than zero.
The signature characters are:
.BR c ,
.BR d ,
.BR D ,
.BR f ,
.BR h ,
.BR i ,
.BR j ,
.BR l ,
.BR p ,
.BR s ,
.BR t ,
.BR z ,
and
.B ?
for an unrecognized specification.
.PP
\f[CB]fmtfs\fP
returns the file system type name corresponding to the
\f[CB]stat\fP
structure
.IR st .
.PP
\f[CB]fmtgid\fP
returns the group name for
.IR gid .
.PP
\f[CB]fmtident\fP
returns the
.BR what (1)
or
.BR ident (1)
identification string contained in
.IR string ,
stripping the leading
\f[CB]@(#)\fP,
\f[CB]$Id:\fP
and
\f[CB][...]\fP
usage markers.
.I string
is returned unchanged if it contains no such markers.
.PP
\f[CB]fmtint\fP
is a performance-optimized function for formatting a base-10 integer
.IR number .
If
.I "unsign != 0"
then the
.IR number
is treated as unsigned.
.PP
\f[CB]fmtip4\fP
formats the host-order IPv4 address
.I addr
in dotted-quad notation.
If
.I bits
is between 0 and 32 then a
.BI / bits
prefix suffix is appended.
.PP
\f[CB]fmtmatch\fP
returns the
\f[CB]strmatch\fP
equivalent pattern for the regular expression pattern
.IR re .
0 is returned for invalid
.IR re .
.PP
\f[CB]fmtmode\fP
returns the
.I "ls \-l"
mode string for the file mode bits in
.IR mode .
If
.I "external != 0"
then
.I mode
is
\f[CB]modecanon\fP(3)
canonical.
.PP
\f[CB]fmtnum\fP
formats
.IR number ,
first divided by
.I divide
if
.IR "divide > 1" ,
as a scaled value of at most 5 characters with a binary
(1024-based) metric suffix selected from
.BR k ,
.B m
and
.BR g .
.PP
\f[CB]fmtperm\fP
returns the
.I chmod
permission string for the permission bits in
.IR perm .
.PP
\f[CB]fmtre\fP
returns the regular expression
equivalent pattern for the
\f[CB]strmatch\fP
pattern
.IR pattern .
0 is returned for invalid
.IR pattern .
.PP
\f[CB]fmtscale\fP
formats
.I number
scaled by
.IR scale ,
which must be 1000 or 1024, using a metric suffix.
The result is at most 5 characters.
For
.I scale
1000 the suffixes are
.BR b ,
.BR k ,
.BR M ,
.BR G ,
.BR T ,
.B P
and
.BR E ;
for 1024 they are
.BR Ki ,
.BR Mi ,
.BR Gi ,
.BR Ti ,
.BR Pi
and
.BR Ei .
.PP
\f[CB]fmtsignal\fP
returns the signal description text for the signal number
.IR sig .
If
.I "sig < 0"
then the signal name, sans
\f[CB]SIG\fP,
for
.I \-sig
is returned.
.PP
\f[CB]fmttime\fP
returns the results of
.I "tmfmt(buf,sizeof(buf),format,tm)"
in the private buffer
.IR buf .
.PP
\f[CB]fmtuid\fP
returns the user name for
.IR uid .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
modecanon(3),
str(3)
